===============================================================================
POLYMORPHISM IN JAVA -- MASTER NOTES
package OOPS.Polymorphism
===============================================================================

Read this file FIRST, then MethodOverloading/Notes, then MethodOverriding/Notes.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. MEANING
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    poly     = many
    morphism = forms / ways to represent
    (both are Greek words)

Polymorphism = ONE NAME, MANY FORMS.

The same method name behaves differently depending on either the arguments
passed to it (decided at compile time) or the actual object it is called on
(decided at runtime).

Real life analogy -- the verb "cut":

        cut(paper)  -> use scissors
        cut(tree)   -> use an axe
        cut(hair)   -> use a trimmer

Same verb. The behaviour is chosen by what you are cutting.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. WHY IT EXISTS  (the real reason, not the textbook reason)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

It lets you write code against a GENERAL type today and have that same code
work with SPECIFIC types you have not written yet.

        void printArea(Shape s) {      // written once, never touched again
            s.area();                   // works for Circle, Square, Rectangle
        }                               // and any Shape you add next year

Without polymorphism you would need a long if-else chain that must be edited
every time a new shape appears:

        if (s instanceof Circle)         { ... }
        else if (s instanceof Square)    { ... }
        else if (s instanceof Rectangle) { ... }   // and edit forever

Polymorphism deletes that chain. This is the "O" in the SOLID principles --
Open for extension, Closed for modification. Every framework you will ever
use (Spring, Collections, JDBC) is built on this one idea.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. THE TWO TYPES -- SIDE BY SIDE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

                        COMPILE-TIME                 RUNTIME
                        POLYMORPHISM                 POLYMORPHISM
  --------------------  ---------------------------  --------------------------
  Also called           Static / Early binding       Dynamic / Late binding
  Achieved by           Method OVERLOADING           Method OVERRIDING
  Who decides           The compiler                 The JVM
  Decided using         Reference & argument types   The real object in memory
  When decided          At compile time              At runtime
  Needs inheritance     No                           Yes -- IS-A is mandatory
  Method name           Same                         Same
  Parameter list        MUST be different            MUST be identical
  Return type           Ignored by the compiler      Same or covariant
  Number of classes     Usually one                  Always two or more
  Works on static       Yes                          No -- that is method HIDING
  Works on private      Yes                          No -- not inherited
  Works on final        Yes                          No -- final blocks it
  Performance           Slightly faster              Slight vtable lookup cost
  Detail notes file     MethodOverloading/Notes      MethodOverriding/Notes

One-line summary to memorise:

    OVERLOADING = same name, DIFFERENT parameters, SAME class, compiler decides.
    OVERRIDING  = same name, SAME parameters, PARENT and CHILD, JVM decides.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. UPCASTING -- THE LINE THAT MAKES RUNTIME POLYMORPHISM POSSIBLE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

        Shape s = new Circle();      // parent reference  <--  child object

This is UPCASTING. Java does it automatically with no cast needed, because
Circle IS-A Shape.

        The REFERENCE decides WHAT you can call   (checked at compile time)
        The OBJECT decides WHICH ONE runs         (chosen at runtime)

Your own ShapesExample.java already shows both halves:

        Circle circle = new Circle();
        circle.area();            // Circle version -- an ordinary call

        ShapesExample shape1 = new Circle();
        shape1.area();            // Circle version -- RUNTIME POLYMORPHISM
                                  // the reference is a ShapesExample but the
                                  // object is a Circle, so Circle.area() runs

DOWNCASTING is the reverse and must be written explicitly, and guarded:

        Shape s = new Circle();
        if (s instanceof Circle) {
            Circle c = (Circle) s;      // safe
        }

Downcasting without the instanceof check risks a ClassCastException at runtime.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. THE THREE CONDITIONS FOR RUNTIME POLYMORPHISM
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

All three must be true. Miss any one and you do not have it:

        1. INHERITANCE        -- an IS-A relationship must exist
        2. METHOD OVERRIDING  -- the child must override the parent's method
        3. UPCASTING          -- a parent reference must hold a child object


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. WHERE POLYMORPHISM SITS AMONG THE FOUR PILLARS OF OOP
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

        1. Encapsulation -- hide the data, expose behaviour (private + getters)
        2. Inheritance   -- reuse behaviour through IS-A       [you have done this]
        3. Polymorphism  -- one name, many forms               [you are here]
        4. Abstraction   -- expose what, hide how (abstract class / interface)

Polymorphism DEPENDS on inheritance, and abstraction is what makes polymorphism
genuinely useful in real code. So after this topic, go straight to abstract
classes and interfaces -- that is where these two ideas join up.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. TERMS THAT ALL MEAN THE SAME THING
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Interviewers switch between these freely. They are synonyms:

    overloading = compile-time polymorphism = static polymorphism
                = static binding = early binding

    overriding  = runtime polymorphism = dynamic polymorphism
                = dynamic binding = late binding = dynamic method dispatch


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
8. THE FIVE MOST COMMON MISTAKES
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    1. Thinking variables are polymorphic. They are NOT. Only instance methods
       are. Fields are resolved from the reference type at compile time.

    2. Thinking static methods can be overridden. They cannot -- that is
       method HIDING and it resolves from the reference type.

    3. Changing the parameter list while trying to override. That silently
       becomes an OVERLOAD instead, and your method never gets called.
       @Override catches this instantly.

    4. Forgetting that the reference type limits which methods are visible.
       Parent p = new Child(); p.childOnlyMethod(); is a compile error.

    5. Assuming a wider return type is allowed. Only NARROWER (covariant)
       return types are legal when overriding.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
9. READING ORDER AND FILES IN THIS PACKAGE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    Notes                          <- you are reading this (overview)
    IntroToPolymorphism.java       <- the definition and the two types
    ShapesExample.java             <- runtime polymorphism in action

    MethodOverloading/Notes        <- rules, resolution order, traps
    MethodOverloading/Sum.java     <- four overloads of sum()
    MethodOverloading/Test.java    <- overloading across parent and child

    MethodOverriding/Notes         <- rules, @Override, super, dispatch, traps
    MethodOverriding/MyTest.java   <- A / B classes proving dynamic dispatch


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
10. HOW TO TEST YOURSELF BEFORE WE START CODING
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Answer these out loud without looking. If you can, you have understood it.

    1. Can you overload a method without inheritance? Can you override one?
    2. Parent p = new Child();  Which class's method runs, and why?
    3. Same line. Can you call a method that only exists in Child? Why not?
    4. Both classes have a field called name. Which one does p.name print?
    5. Both show() methods are static. Which one runs, and what is it called?
    6. Parent throws IOException. Can the child throw Exception? SQLException?
       FileNotFoundException? Nothing at all?
    7. Parent method is protected. Can the child make it private? public?
    8. Why does @Override matter if the JVM does not need it?
    9. Name the three conditions required for runtime polymorphism.
   10. Which is decided by the compiler and which by the JVM?
